  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The current implementation uses local optimization with SLSQP, which may easily get trapped in local minima. Our approach addresses this by:\n   - Using multi-start optimization from diverse starting points\n   - Utilizing random noise perturbations to explore the search space\n   - Maintaining the same n_points=280 resolution as the parent solution\n   - Projecting the parent solution through random noise perturbations followed by constraint satisfaction\n\n   This method systematically explores the solution space around the parent solution and other starting points, providing a more global search capability. We focus on three key improvements:\n   - More starts for better exploration\n   - Better projection to maintain feasibility\n   - More aggressive noise for divergence\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Objective function: max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx implemented as:\n        max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')) * dx\n   - Constraints:\n        0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n        sum(h) = 140\n   - Variables: \n        h: array of 280 real numbers\n        dx=2/280\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   Step 1: Setup parameters\n        n_points=280\n        dx=2.0/n_points\n        integral_target=140\n        num_starts=12\n\n   Step 2: Generate multiple starting points\n        for i in range(num_starts):\n            if i==0:\n                Start 1: Parent with noise (large amplitude to diversify)\n            else:\n                Start i: Random uniform in [0,1] grid\n\n   Step 3: For each start:\n        Clip values to [0,1]\n        Use SLSQP with 1500 max iterations to optimize against constraints\n\n   Step 4: Keep best valid solution (lowest objective)\n\n   Step 5: Projection and repair after each start\n        Repair by:\n            Adjusting toward 0.5 if far from constraint\n            Scaling if needed to satisfy sum=140\n            Clipping to ensure [0,1] bounds\n\n   Step 6: Evaluation\n        For each candidate: \n            Clip values\n            Compute correlation over all lags\n            Return max correlation * dx\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Uses random noise perturbations (12 starts)\n   - Projecting toward feasible region\n   - Aggressive initial perturbations (10% of initial amplitudes)\n   - Values projected locally if violated\n\n5. Compute budget:\n   - Dominant operation: correlation (O(n^2) for 280 points)\n   - 12\u00d71500=18000 function evaluations\n   - Fast (few tenths of second in Python)\n   - Time budget safe (all available)\n\n6. Validation:\n   - Clip all violations to [0,1]\n   - Check sum close to 140 within 1% tolerance\n   - Return full evaluation as before",
